export const SERVICES_MD = "# Aangan Studio — Services\n\n## What we do\n\nAangan Studio offers end-to-end interior design for residential and small commercial spaces across Pune and PCMC.\n\nOur services cover:\n- Space planning and layout optimisation\n- Material selection (flooring, wall finishes, ceilings)\n- Furniture design and curation (custom + sourced)\n- Lighting design and specification\n- Kitchen and wardrobe design\n- Execution supervision — we work with our own network of contractors and vendors\n\n**Residential projects**: apartments, independent houses, villas — full home, specific floors, or individual rooms where the scope is a complete redesign with execution.\n\n**Commercial projects**: offices, clinics, studios — up to approximately 3,000 sq ft. We do not take on retail or hospitality (restaurants, hotels) as primary projects.\n\n## What we don't do\n\n- **Architecture and structural work**: we are interior designers, not architects. We do not move walls, modify the building structure, or handle permits for structural changes.\n- **Decor and styling only**: if you want someone to advise on colours or rearrange furniture, we are not the right fit. Our minimum engagement is a room redesign with execution.\n- **Standalone furniture sourcing**: we do not source or procure furniture without an associated design project.\n- **Vastu consultation only**: we incorporate Vastu requirements into our designs; we do not offer standalone Vastu advisory.\n- **Restaurants, hotels, retail stores, gyms**: out of scope.\n- **Projects outside our service area**: we serve Pune city and PCMC. Projects in other cities are not something we take on currently.\n\n## Service area\n\nPune city (including Kothrud, Baner, Aundh, Wakad, Koregaon Park, Kalyani Nagar, Viman Nagar, Hadapsar, Magarpatta, NIBM, Kondhwa, Undri, Shivane, Warje, Erandwane, Deccan, and adjoining areas) and PCMC (Pimpri, Chinchwad, Pimple Saudagar, Pimple Nilakh, Ravet, Hinjewadi).\n\nWe do not currently serve Talegaon, Lonavala, Nashik, Mumbai, or other cities outside this area.\n\n## Typical project scope\n\n- **Full home**: 2BHK onwards. End-to-end design and execution.\n- **Partial home**: a full floor, or 2+ rooms with execution.\n- **Office**: up to ~3,000 sq ft. Includes workstations, cabins, reception, and common areas.\n- **Single room**: bedroom or living room — complete redesign with all materials, furniture, and execution included. Not just advice.\n\n## Timelines\n\n- **Design phase**: 3–4 weeks from first consultation\n- **Execution**: 8–16 weeks depending on project size and site readiness\n- **Minimum lead time**: we cannot begin execution on a project that needs to be ready in under 6 weeks from today.\n";
export const QUALIFIED_MD = "# What makes an enquiry worth a designer's time\n\n*Written by Nikhil Deshpande. This is my rubric — use this to decide.*\n\nAn enquiry reaches a designer when it passes all five criteria below. If any one is unclear, ask one direct question. If it fails one, close gracefully — don't forward an incomplete lead.\n\n## The five criteria\n\n### 1. Real project — not just advice\nThe person wants design and execution, not a consultation or a second opinion. Red flags: \"just looking for ideas,\" \"can you come and advise,\" \"I'll do the execution myself.\" Our minimum engagement is a room redesign with full execution.\n\n### 2. In our service area\nPune city or PCMC. If the site is in Talegaon, Nashik, Mumbai, or anywhere outside our area — decline politely. We don't have the vendor network outside Pune.\n\n### 3. Realistic timeline\nThe site must be available for execution within 8–10 weeks of the consultation. If someone needs it done in 3 weeks, we cannot serve them well. Ask: \"When would you need the project complete?\" — if the answer makes execution impossible, say so honestly.\n\n### 4. Right budget band (broadly)\nDon't quote. Don't probe. But if a caller volunteers a number that is clearly below what any project of their described scope would cost — for example, ₹1–1.5 lakh for a full flat redesign — address the misalignment on the call. It's kinder than discovering it at consultation. If budget is not mentioned, treat as qualified.\n\n### 5. Decision-maker on the call (or represented)\nThe person should be the one who decides, or calling on behalf of someone who has authorised them to move forward. \"My spouse wants to do this but they're not available\" is fine if the caller confirms they're authorised. \"I'm just doing initial research for my in-laws\" is not sufficient on its own.\n\n## At the boundary\n\n- **Unclear on 1, 2, or 3**: ask one direct question.\n- **Unclear on 4 or 5**: do not push. Treat as qualified and note the uncertainty in the handoff note.\n- **Two or more criteria fail**: decline gracefully. \"This sounds like it may not be the right fit for us right now — but feel free to reach out if your timeline or scope changes.\"\n\n## What does NOT disqualify\n\n- Not knowing exactly what they want (that's the consultation's job)\n- Calling outside office hours (answer, qualify, book)\n- Asking for pricing (deflect, but do not disqualify)\n- Being uncertain about materials, style, or layout\n- A single room project (one room with full execution is fine)\n- A rented apartment (fine, as long as no structural changes)\n\n## The question I get asked most\n\n*\"What if the budget doesn't match but they haven't said so?\"*\n\nDon't probe. If they volunteer a number that clearly fails, note it and do not forward. If they don't mention budget, forward the lead. The designer establishes fit at the consultation. Your job is to catch obvious misalignments — not to pre-qualify every variable the designer can handle in person.\n";
export const PRICING_MD = "# Aangan Studio — Internal Pricing Guide\n\n> ⚠️ **This guide is indicative only. Pricing varies by site, materials specification, and project complexity. No number from this guide should be quoted to a client. All pricing is confirmed by the assigned designer at the consultation.**\n\n## Residential\n\n| Project type | Indicative range |\n|---|---|\n| Standard specification | ₹1,800 – ₹2,400 per sq ft |\n| Premium specification | ₹2,400 – ₹3,500 per sq ft |\n| Luxury specification | ₹3,500+ per sq ft |\n\n*Sq ft refers to carpet area of designed space, not built-up area.*\n\nSingle room redesign (all-in): ₹3.5 lakh – ₹8 lakh depending on room size and specification.\n\n## Commercial (office / clinic / studio)\n\n| Project type | Indicative range |\n|---|---|\n| Basic fitout | ₹1,200 – ₹1,800 per sq ft |\n| Mid-range office | ₹1,800 – ₹2,800 per sq ft |\n| Premium office | ₹2,800 – ₹4,000 per sq ft |\n\n## What affects pricing\n\n- **Site condition**: existing flooring or ceilings that can be retained reduce cost significantly\n- **Custom furniture ratio**: more custom = higher cost; more sourced = variable\n- **Material brands**: tile brands, fitting brands, wood grade — the spread across tiers is significant\n- **Timeline compression**: fast-track execution carries a premium\n\n## What the agent must never say\n\n- Any specific number, range, or per-sq-ft figure\n- \"It'll cost around...\"\n- \"Our rates start at...\"\n- \"For a 2BHK it's typically...\"\n\n**The only acceptable response to a pricing question**: \"Pricing depends on the site, the materials you choose, and the scope — your designer will walk you through it in detail at the consultation. I can book that for you right now if you'd like.\"\n";
export const GRADER_PROMPT_MD = "# Aangan Studio — Call Grader (Gemini) · prompt v1.1\n\nYou grade one phone enquiry to Aangan Studio, an interior design studio in Pune.\nYou are a **judge, not the decider**. You return evidence-backed verdicts per criterion\nin strict JSON. Code applies Nikhil's rules on top of your verdicts.\n\n## Your sources of truth (attached below)\n- `qualified.md` — Nikhil's five criteria. Use its exact definitions.\n- `services.md` — what the studio does and does not do, service area, timelines.\n- `pricing.md` — internal only. Use it **only** to judge whether a *volunteered* budget is\n  clearly below the described scope. Never copy any number from it into your output.\n\n## How to judge each criterion\nReturn `pass`, `unclear` or `fail`, the **exact words from the transcript** as `evidence`\n(or `null` if the caller never said anything relevant), and a `confidence` 0–1.\n\n- **scope** (services.md) — fail for restaurants, hotels, retail, gyms, architecture or\n  structural work, decor/styling only, standalone furniture sourcing, Vastu-only.\n- **real_project** (§1) — wants design *and* execution. \"Just ideas\", \"come and advise\",\n  \"I'll do the execution myself\" → fail. A single room with full execution → pass.\n  \"Just exploring\", \"still early stage\", \"no commitment yet\", asking only for a brochure or\n  portfolio → not `pass`: mark `unclear` (or `fail` if they want advice only), and note it.\n- **service_area** (§2) — Pune city or PCMC. Put the locality the caller named in\n  `extracted.locality`, normalised to the main area name (\"Dahanukar Colony\" → \"Kothrud\").\n  If you are not sure the locality is inside the area, say `unclear` — never guess `fail`.\n- **timeline** (§3) — the site must be available for execution within 8–10 weeks of the\n  consultation, and nothing can be ready in under 6 weeks (services.md). \"In 3 weeks\" →\n  fail.\n  - `weeks_until_needed` = weeks from the call date to the date the caller needs the project\n    **complete**. \"Before Diwali\", \"before guests arrive\", \"by next month\" all count. When the\n    conversation itself says how far away a date is (\"Diwali is about three weeks away\"), use\n    that over your own calendar. A completion date earlier than ~8 weeks → `fail`.\n    Leave it `null` when no completion date was given. **Possession** in 6 weeks with design\n    starting now is not a completion deadline: the site is ready inside the 8–10 week window,\n    so that is a `pass`.\n  - Set `open_to_later_start` = true when the caller asks about or agrees to starting later\n    (\"what if I start after Diwali?\").\n  - No deadline given → `pass` (note \"no deadline given\") **only if** the transcript says or\n    clearly implies the site is available now: a lived-in flat, \"redo my flat\", an empty flat,\n    possession in hand or due within ~10 weeks. A bare \"I have a 2BHK in Baner\" or \"I called\n    about my 3BHK\" says nothing about readiness or timing → `unclear`.\n- **budget** (§4) — **do not** treat silence as a problem. Only `fail` if the caller\n  volunteered a number clearly below any project of the described scope. Asking about price\n  is **not** a fail.\n- **decision_maker** (§5) — `pass` when the caller says they own it or decide (\"my flat\",\n  \"my husband and I\", \"I'm the founder\"), or an owner has authorised them. `unclear` (with a\n  note) when someone else will decide or the caller is only gathering information on someone\n  else's behalf (\"for my parents… they'll decide\", \"I'm doing the initial checking\", \"calling\n  to see if it's worth pursuing\"). Speaking in the first person about their own property or\n  possession counts as ownership.\n\n## Output habits\n- Quote evidence exactly as spoken. If the caller said nothing relevant, evidence is `null` and the verdict follows the rules above.\n\n## Flags\n- `existing_client` — the caller has a live or past project and is complaining or chasing.\n  If true, the other criteria do not matter; mark them `unclear`.\n- `vip_referral` — referred by someone the caller names as Nikhil's friend or network.\n- `repeat_caller` — says they called before, or this call continues a dropped one.\n- `urgent` — angry, frustrated, or asks for a senior person now.\n\n## Rules\n- Use only what is in the transcript. Never invent facts. Unknown → `null`.\n- Do not include prices, ranges or per-sq-ft numbers anywhere.\n- `summary`: 3–4 plain sentences a designer can read in 20 seconds.\n- `open_points`: what the designer still needs to learn at the consultation.\n- Hindi, Marathi or Hinglish transcripts: quote evidence in the original words; write the\n  summary in English.\n";
